AI SEARCH LENS — PRODUCT REQUIREMENTS DOCUMENT
=================================================

Project
-------
TinyFish Technical Student Bounty Drop 001

Product Name
------------
AI Search Lens

Product Type
------------
SEO + AI Search Visibility Page Auditor

Document Status
---------------
MVP Product Requirements

1. PRODUCT VISION
-----------------

Build an evidence-backed auditor that evaluates how well any publicly accessible webpage can be discovered, read, understood, and potentially cited by search engines and AI systems.

The product must audit the EXACT page submitted by the user.

This is a page-level product, not a homepage/domain-only checker.

Core promise:

"Paste any page. See how visible and AI-readable it is — and exactly what to fix."

2. PROBLEM
----------

Traditional SEO tools often overwhelm users with technical metrics or focus heavily on domains.

AI search introduces another requirement:

A page can technically exist and be indexable while still being difficult for AI systems to understand, extract, and confidently cite.

Users need one simple workflow that connects:

- Search discoverability
- Technical SEO
- Content/intent
- AI readability
- Citation readiness
- Actionable fixes

3. TARGET USERS
---------------

Primary:

- Developers
- Student builders
- Startup founders
- SEO beginners
- Content creators
- SaaS teams
- Marketing teams

Secondary:

- SEO consultants
- Growth teams
- Agencies
- Technical marketers

4. CORE USER STORY
------------------

As a user, I want to paste any public webpage URL so that I can understand whether that exact page is discoverable in search and readable/understandable by AI systems, and receive prioritized fixes.

5. NON-NEGOTIABLE REQUIREMENTS
------------------------------

5.1 Any URL

The input must accept:

- example.com
- www.example.com
- https://example.com
- example.com/pricing
- example.com/blog/article
- example.com/docs/getting-started
- example.com/product/tool

The system must NOT force the user to submit only a root domain.

5.2 Exact page auditing

If the user enters:

example.com/pricing

the system must audit:

/pricing

It must not silently replace it with:

example.com/

5.3 URL normalization

The system should safely normalize incomplete URLs.

Example:

example.com/pricing

becomes:

https://example.com/pricing

5.4 TinyFish Fetch

TinyFish Fetch must read the live submitted page.

5.5 TinyFish Search

TinyFish Search must be used to gather meaningful discovery/search context.

5.6 Evidence-backed output

Important findings must be based on observed page/search evidence.

5.7 Actionable recommendations

The report must tell users what to change, not merely identify problems.

5.8 Visibility + AI readability

The report must explicitly connect search visibility with AI readability.

6. USER FLOW
------------

STEP 1 — Landing

User sees:

AI Search Lens

Headline:

"Audit any webpage for Search + AI visibility."

Input:

"example.com/pricing"

Button:

"Audit Page →"

Optional query field:

"Optional: what would someone search to find this page?"

STEP 2 — Validation

System:

- trims input
- validates URL
- adds protocol if missing
- rejects unsupported schemes
- checks public accessibility constraints
- preserves path/query information

STEP 3 — Audit Initialization

The system stores:

targetUrl
normalizedUrl
optionalQuery

The target URL becomes immutable for the audit run.

STEP 4 — Live Page Fetch

TinyFish Fetch reads the exact URL.

STEP 5 — Search Discovery

TinyFish Search investigates relevant search/discovery context.

STEP 6 — Analysis

Analysis engine evaluates:

- technical SEO
- content
- intent
- search visibility
- AI readability
- citation readiness

STEP 7 — Scoring

System generates:

- overall score
- category scores
- severity levels
- evidence
- recommendations

STEP 8 — Results

User receives an actionable report.

7. AUDIT CATEGORIES
-------------------

A. Technical SEO — 25%

Evaluate:

- page accessibility
- HTTPS
- title
- meta description
- canonical
- robots/indexability signals
- heading structure
- internal links
- image alt text
- structured data
- important technical issues

B. Search Visibility — 25%

Evaluate using TinyFish Search:

- discoverability
- query alignment
- result presence
- search snippet quality
- competing pages
- search intent alignment

C. AI Readability — 25%

Evaluate:

- clarity of page purpose
- information hierarchy
- headings
- concise definitions
- structured content
- important facts
- entity clarity
- extractability
- ambiguity
- excessive boilerplate

D. Content & Intent — 15%

Evaluate:

- primary topic
- target audience
- intent alignment
- completeness
- useful information
- content quality
- unnecessary fluff

E. Citation Readiness — 10%

Evaluate whether an AI system could:

- understand the information
- identify useful claims
- extract them
- attribute them to the page
- confidently cite the page

8. SCORE
--------

Overall score: 0–100.

Suggested weighting:

Technical SEO        25
Search Visibility    25
AI Readability       25
Content & Intent     15
Citation Readiness   10

Every score deduction must be explainable.

Do not produce arbitrary scores.

9. FINDING FORMAT
-----------------

Every important finding should contain:

- Title
- Category
- Severity
- Evidence
- Why it matters
- Recommended fix
- Expected impact
- Confidence

Example:

HIGH — Weak page definition

Evidence:
The page's main purpose is not clearly stated near the primary heading.

Why it matters:
AI systems may have lower confidence about what the page represents.

Fix:
Add a concise definition/value proposition directly below the H1.

10. SEVERITY
------------

CRITICAL
Blocks access, crawling, indexing, or fundamental understanding.

HIGH
Meaningful issue with strong expected visibility/readability impact.

MEDIUM
Important optimization opportunity.

LOW
Minor improvement or polish.

11. REPORT STRUCTURE
--------------------

Header:

AI Search Lens

[Exact submitted URL]

Overall Score:
74/100

Summary:
Short explanation of the page's current state.

Category cards:

Search Visibility
AI Readability
Technical SEO
Content & Intent
Citation Readiness

Priority Fixes:

1. High-impact issue
2. High-impact issue
3. Medium issue
4. Medium issue
5. Low issue

Evidence:

Show the observed evidence supporting important findings.

Visibility ↔ AI Readability:

Explain:

- whether the page can be discovered
- whether the content is easy to understand
- whether AI systems can extract useful information
- whether the page is citation-ready

Exact Page Details:

Display:

- target URL
- title
- meta description
- canonical
- headings
- structured data
- relevant technical signals

12. OPTIONAL QUERY
------------------

A user may enter a search query.

Example:

URL:
example.com/pricing

Query:
best AI resume analyzer for students

The query should influence Search and intent analysis.

If no query is supplied:

Infer likely intent from the page.

The query is optional and must never be required for an audit.

13. TINYFISH REQUIREMENT
-----------------------

TinyFish is a core part of the product.

The implementation must demonstrate meaningful usage of:

1. TinyFish Fetch
2. TinyFish Search

Fetch proves what the submitted page actually contains.

Search provides discovery/visibility context.

The final demo must make this relationship understandable.

14. EXACT PAGE PRINCIPLE
------------------------

Submitted URL:

https://site.com/blog/article

Primary audit:

/blog/article

Supporting context may include:

site.com

But the audit must never become a domain-only audit.

This requirement is critical to product correctness.

15. ERROR STATES
----------------

Invalid URL:

"Enter a valid public webpage URL, such as example.com/pricing."

Unreachable page:

"We couldn't access this page. Check that the URL is public and reachable."

TinyFish Fetch failure:

"Page reading failed. No fabricated findings will be generated."

TinyFish Search failure:

"Search visibility could not be verified. Other page-level checks are still available."

Partial audit:

Clearly mark which sections were not verified.

16. SECURITY REQUIREMENTS
-------------------------

The backend must protect against arbitrary URL abuse.

Required:

- SSRF protection
- localhost/private-network blocking
- protocol restrictions
- request timeout
- response-size limits
- rate limiting
- API keys server-side only
- safe HTML handling
- controlled browser execution

17. PERFORMANCE REQUIREMENTS
----------------------------

Target total audit duration:

Preferably under 60 seconds.

Where safe, Fetch and Search may run concurrently.

The system must prioritize reliable evidence over fake speed/progress.

18. MVP ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA
---------------------------

The MVP passes when all are true:

[ ] Root URL works
[ ] Nested page URL works
[ ] Blog page works
[ ] Pricing page works
[ ] Product page works
[ ] Documentation page works
[ ] URL without https:// works
[ ] Exact submitted URL is preserved
[ ] Exact page is fetched
[ ] TinyFish Fetch is used meaningfully
[ ] TinyFish Search is used meaningfully
[ ] Overall score is 0–100
[ ] Category scores exist
[ ] Findings contain evidence
[ ] Findings contain severity
[ ] Recommendations are actionable
[ ] Visibility and AI readability are both evaluated
[ ] Relationship between visibility and readability is explained
[ ] Failed live checks are not fabricated
[ ] Partial failures are clearly disclosed

19. DEMO REQUIREMENT
-------------------

The final demonstration should use multiple real public pages.

Suggested validation set:

- homepage
- pricing page
- blog article
- product page
- documentation page

For each page:

1. Submit exact URL.
2. Confirm the target page is preserved.
3. Confirm TinyFish Fetch reads that page.
4. Confirm TinyFish Search provides discovery context.
5. Confirm findings are specific to the page.
6. Confirm recommendations are actionable.

The demo must visibly prove that the application is an actual PAGE AUDITOR.

20. SUCCESS METRICS
-------------------

MVP success:

- Users can audit any public page without confusion.
- Results are understandable within seconds.
- Findings are evidence-backed.
- Recommendations are actionable.
- TinyFish Search + Fetch are meaningfully integrated.
- The product demonstrates a clear Search ↔ AI readability connection.

21. PRODUCT NORTH STAR
----------------------

The product should answer four questions:

1. Can search systems discover this page?
2. Can crawlers read this page?
3. Can AI systems understand this page?
4. Can AI systems confidently extract and cite information from this page?

Final positioning:

"AI Search Lens turns any public webpage into an evidence-backed Search + AI visibility audit."

END OF PRD
